Here is a complete example for a TypeScript runner:
```typescript
class Shelf<T> {
  private values: T[] = [];
  add(value: T): void {
    this.values.push(value);
  }
  first(): T | undefined {
    return this.values[0];
  }
}
const counts = new Shelf<number>();
counts.add(11);
counts.add(22);
console.log(counts.first());
```
The output is 11. T[] means an array whose elements have type T. [] creates an empty array. push appends a value. [0] reads the first element because array positions begin at zero.

Continue after the Shelf class:
```typescript
const colors = new Shelf<string>();
colors.add('amber');
const empty = new Shelf<number>();
const maybeCount = empty.first();
console.log(colors.first());
console.log(maybeCount);
if (maybeCount !== undefined) {
  console.log(maybeCount + 1);
}
```
The outputs are amber and undefined; the guarded addition does not run. !== means not equal. The check narrows maybeCount to number inside the branch, making arithmetic safe. A present value of 0 would pass this check.

